This fixture is DISTRIBUTION-ONLY. It must never be copied to a consumer.

Its SUBJECT does reach a consumer: install.sh:331 copies
core/skills/ai-dlc-update/reconcile/* to .claude/skills/ai-dlc-update/reconcile/, so
settings-merge.sh is on every installed tree. By the criterion in
.claude/rules/fixture-ship-decl.md that alone would make this fixture SHIP. It does not
ship, and the reason is the CORPUS rather than the subject.

The decisive assertion here is the POSITIVE control: the correct template must still reach
`model_row_needed=yes`. Without it the fixture passes against a script that answers `no` to
everything -- a "fix" that closes the check by breaking it. That control needs a template a
real producer emitted, and the only one is templates/settings.json.template.

MEASURED: `settings.json.template` occurs exactly ONCE in scripts/install.sh, at :408, and
that occurrence is an ASSIGNMENT reading it out of the distribution
(`$SCRIPT_DIR/../templates/`). No `cp` puts it under `$PROJECT_ROOT`. The control in the same
invocation is coding-conventions.md.template, which does appear on a `cp ... "$PROJECT_ROOT/..."`
line. The two documented invocation sites agree: SKILL.md:879 and apply.sh:1252 both reach the
template as `git -C <dist> show <theirs>:templates/settings.json.template`, a git object in the
distribution clone, never a path in the consumer tree.

So on a consumer this fixture's positive control has nothing to point --template at. It would
either abort or degrade to asserting only that unparseable templates are refused -- which is
the half that passes against a script saying `no` to everything.

THAT FAILURE IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL. core/fixtures/settings-merge-documented-form/.dist-only
records the same template dependency shipping without a marker in 0.151.0 and blocking the
reference consumer's self-update for three consecutive releases while the distribution suite
stayed green. Shipping the template alongside the fixture is the wrong repair for the same
reason given there: a consumer has no use for the distribution's settings template, and adding
a file to satisfy a test is how the test stops describing anything.

The second, independent reason is the third measured shape in .claude/rules/fixture-ship-decl.md:
this is a MUTATION BATTERY. It writes four mutated copies of core's own settings-merge.sh into
a mktemp directory and scores each against the same arms. A consumer has no use for a battery
over a file it does not author and that every pull overwrites wholesale.
